// newfonts-demo — three bundled faces added alongside the originals: EB Garamond (a Garamond revival for
// Latin body text), Amiri (a traditional Naskh book hand for Arabic), and Ezra SIL (the scholarly Biblical
// Hebrew face). Each is selected by name with \font: \font ebgaramond 12, \font amiri 14, \font ezra 14.

\use{document}

\title{Three New Faces}
\author{The texish engine}
\date{Version 0.26.0}
\maketitle

\section{EB Garamond}

{\font ebgaramond 12
EB Garamond is a revival of the sixteenth-century roman of Claude Garamont, a quiet book face for running
text. The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. It carries {\font ebgaramond 12 italic an italic},
{\font ebgaramond 12 medium a medium}, {\font ebgaramond 12 semibold a semibold}, {\font ebgaramond 12 bold a
bold} and {\font ebgaramond 12 extrabold an extrabold} weight, with the f-ligatures — fi, fl, ffi — and proper
curly ``quotes'' and dashes.
}

\section{Amiri --- Arabic}

Amiri is a traditional Naskh book hand in the Bulaq tradition, an alternative to the bundled Noto Naskh. The
cursive joining and the right-to-left reordering are the engine's, exactly as for the other Arabic face.

\medskip

\rtl
{\font amiri 16 regular
اللغة العربية من أقدم اللغات في العالم. تكتب من اليمين إلى اليسار، وتتصل حروفها بعضها
ببعض داخل الكلمة الواحدة.
}
\ltr

\section{Ezra SIL --- Hebrew}

Ezra SIL is cut in the style of the Biblia Hebraica Stuttgartensia, the face a critical edition of the Hebrew
Bible is set in. It carries the consonants, the vowel points and the cantillation marks.

\medskip

\rtl
{\font ezra 16 regular
בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ׃ וְהָאָרֶץ הָיְתָה תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ
וְחֹשֶׁךְ עַל־פְּנֵי תְהוֹם׃
}
\ltr

\vfill\eject
